        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: It Comes Down to Sheen
          </h2>

          <p>
            Foundation Armor describes the AR350 as a solvent-based low gloss pure acrylic sealer and the AR500 as a solvent-based high gloss pure acrylic sealer. Both are built to seal, darken and protect concrete and concrete pavers, both are marketed for driveways, walkways, patios and pool decks, and both leave a surface film rather than soaking in. If you lay the two technical data sheets next to each other, the only line in the technical information block that changes is the dry appearance: low gloss for the AR350, high gloss for the AR500.
          </p>

          <p>
            That matters because a lot of what gets written about these two products assumes the AR500 is the heavier, higher-solids sealer and the AR350 is the lighter one. The current data sheets do not support that. Both list solids at 25 percent, both claim up to 175 square feet per gallon per coat on porous surfaces and 200 to 225 on smooth, prepped ones, and both carry the same drying, recoat and traffic times. The number in the name is not a solids rating you can compare, so do not buy the AR500 expecting a thicker or longer-lasting film on the strength of the bigger number.
          </p>

          <p>
            What you are actually choosing between is a satin-style wet look and a glossy wet look. Both will darken the concrete in the same way, and Foundation Armor uses near-identical wording for each, warning that the sealer will dramatically darken and enhance the colour of the substrate. The AR500 then adds a more reflective surface on top of that colour change. On a smooth, well-coloured stamped pattern that reflection can look spectacular. On a broom-finished driveway it can look like a plastic coating, and it makes every scuff, tyre path and patch of uneven absorption easier to see.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the decision is less about protection and more about appearance and safety. If the surface is walked on wet, sits in full sun, or has a rough texture meant to look like natural stone, the AR350 is the easier product to live with. If the surface is smooth, under cover or mostly decorative, and you want maximum shine, the AR500 is the one built for that. Our full <a href="/armor-ar350-concrete-sealer-review/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">Armor AR350 review</a> goes deeper on the low-gloss version; this page sticks to the head-to-head.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE VERDICT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Same chemistry, same coverage, same lifespan claim. Choose by gloss.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Foundation Armor's 2025 data sheets list 25% solids, 175-225 sq ft per gallon, 24-hour foot traffic and 48-hour wheel traffic for both. The AR350 dries to low gloss and the AR500 to high gloss, and that is the decision.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="spec-sheets" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. AR350 vs AR500 Spec Sheets, Side by Side
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every figure in the table below comes from Foundation Armor's own technical data sheets and product pages for the two sealers, as published in 2025. We have not lab-tested either product, so treat these as the maker's ratings rather than independent measurements. Where the maker gives a range, we have kept the range rather than picking a flattering end of it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
