                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No film to wear, slip or peel</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you cannot decide, do what Foundation Armor itself suggests and test. Buy the 16-ounce sample of each, clean and dry a corner of the slab properly, and apply two thin coats of each side by side in the evening. Look at the result after a few days, in sun and in shade, wet and dry. The panel you still like after a week is your answer, and the cost of two samples is trivial next to sealing an entire patio in a sheen you end up disliking.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if neither sheen appeals once you see it, that is useful information too. A penetrating silane or siloxane sealer protects against water and deicing salt without changing the appearance, lasts longer between applications and cannot peel. Our <a href="/best-stamped-concrete-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best stamped concrete sealer roundup</a> ranks both film-forming and penetrating options, so you can compare these two against everything else before buying.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the AR500 for Extra Durability</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The bigger number does not mean a thicker or longer-lasting film. Foundation Armor's data sheets list 25% solids and the same 1 to 3 year exterior life for both sealers. If you want more protection, apply two proper coats or look at a penetrating sealer; do not pay for shine expecting it to buy you years.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing in the Morning or Midday Sun</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both data sheets say to apply only in the early evening, when temperatures are falling and the sun is off the slab. Midday application on hot concrete flashes the solvent off too fast and causes bubbles and haze, and morning dew causes blushing. Plan the job around an evening with a dry night forecast.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping a Test Patch on Broom-Finished Concrete</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Foundation Armor strongly suggests a test area on surfaces without colour variation, because the wet look darkens plain grey concrete dramatically and the AR500 adds glassy reflection on top. Many owners discover they dislike it only after sealing the whole driveway. A 16-ounce sample answers the question for very little money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying Over an Incompatible Sealer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Neither product will bond over a water-based acrylic, acrylic latex, paint, epoxy or urethane. Applied over one, both films can fail together. Check what is already on the slab with a solvent test or the original receipts, and strip anything incompatible with a remover such as CR100 before you start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Out the Non-Slip on Walking Surfaces</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both sealers can be slippery when wet, and the high-gloss AR500 offers even less texture underfoot. Mixing ultra-fine grit into the sealer, or broadcasting fine grit into the second coat and back-rolling, costs little and changes the look only slightly. Pool decks, steps and slopes should never be sealed without it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between Armor AR350 and AR500?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                According to Foundation Armor's technical data sheets, the only listed difference is the finish: the AR350 dries to a low-gloss wet look and the AR500 to a high-gloss wet look. Both are solvent-based pure acrylics with 25% solids, the same 175 to 225 square feet per gallon coverage, the same dry and traffic times and the same 1 to 3 year exterior life claim.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the AR500 have more solids than the AR350?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not according to the current data sheets. Foundation Armor's 2025 technical data sheets list solids at 25% for both products. Older forum posts and some reviews describe the AR500 as the higher-solids option, but the published specs show the same figure, which is why both carry identical coverage and recoat guidance. The difference you can see is gloss, not film thickness.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is AR350 or AR500 better for stamped concrete?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are approved for stamped concrete. The AR350's low gloss usually looks more natural on textured patterns that imitate stone, and it hides wear and minor flaws better. The AR500's high gloss suits smooth patterns with strong colour where you want maximum shine. On stamped pool decks and steps, use the AR350 with a non-slip additive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put AR500 over AR350, or the other way round?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Both are solvent-based acrylics, and Foundation Armor says each can be applied to surfaces previously sealed with a solvent-based acrylic. That means you can add gloss later by recoating an AR350 slab with AR500, or tone down an AR500 finish with AR350. Clean and dry the surface first and test a small area, because remaining film affects the final sheen.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do the AR350 and AR500 last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Foundation Armor claims up to 1 to 3 years of life on exterior surfaces before recoating and up to 2 to 7 years on interior surfaces, for both products. Real life depends on traffic, sun, porosity and how much resin has built up. Driveways usually sit at the shorter end; covered and lightly used areas at the longer end.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Armor AR350 and AR500 slippery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both can be slippery when wet, and the maker's data sheets say so. Foundation Armor recommends its non-slip additive where slipperiness is a concern: ultra-fine grit drill-mixed at 3 to 6.5 ounces per gallon, or fine and coarse grit broadcast into the second coat and back-rolled. The glossier AR500 generally offers less texture underfoot, so grit matters even more with it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use AR350 or AR500 on natural stone or brick?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Foundation Armor says neither should be used on clay brick, red Chicago pavers, flagstone, slate or natural stone, and recommends its penetrating WL550 or SX5000 WB for those surfaces instead. Neither should be applied to pavers laid over a concrete slab or concrete base, which the maker says causes ongoing blushing and delamination.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

